# [MANET](../wg/manet.html)

## Summary

The Mobile Ad Hoc Networks (MANET) working group met to review working group document status, discuss `draft-ietf-manet-inet-gap-analysis`, and address procedural and architectural feedback. Publication has been requested for the physical layer DLEP extension drafts. Updates were provided on `draft-ietf-manet-olsrv2-responsive` following Routing Directorate review. The primary discussion focused on `draft-ietf-manet-inet-gap-analysis`, including concerns regarding its title, separation of problem statements from solution proposals, alignment with the WG charter, and resolving unaddressed comments from its adoption call. The session concluded with open microphone discussions on non-terrestrial MANET scenarios, DTN interaction, and the status of the expired AODV draft.

## Key Discussion Points

### Session Intro and Document Status
* Presented using [IETF 127 MANET session chairs slides](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-manet-ietf-127-manet-session-chairs-slides-00) by Don Fedyk and Ronald in 't Velt.
* **Physical Layer / DLEP Drafts**: Publication has been requested for the physical layer modem-to-router drafts (dash-05 versions) as of November 15, and they are now awaiting IESG and IETF Last Call processing.
* **`draft-ietf-manet-olsrv2-responsive`**: Version -02 was submitted following an early review by the Routing Directorate. Working group participants were requested to review the draft. A document shepherd is required; Ronald in 't Velt volunteered if no other participant steps forward.
* **Individual Drafts**:
  * Donald Eastlake reported plans to address comments from Juliusz Chroboczek and post a revised version of his Babel in 802.11 draft before the next IETF meeting.
  * Feedback on the mailing list was requested for Fraunhofer FKIE individual drafts (MANET awareness model and DLEP extension).

### MANET Internetworking Architecture, Problem Statement and Gap Analysis
* Presented using [MANET Internetworking Architecture, Problem Statement and Gap Analysis](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-manet-manet-internetworking-architecture-problem-statement-and-gap-analysis-00) by Fred Templin.
* **Content**: The presentation covered Layer 2 versus Layer 3 MANET dynamics, virtual mobile overlay interfaces, scalable provider-independent addressing without aggregation, and proposals to incorporate solution analyses (e.g., AERO/OMNI).
* **Document Scope and Title**: Abdussalam Baryun voiced opposition to changing the document title to include "Architecture," emphasizing that the draft should focus primarily on problems and gap analysis.
* **Separation of Problem Statement and Solutions**: Ronald in 't Velt noted that solution-specific elements (e.g., MLAs and MSPs) are currently interwoven into the text and recommended separating potential solutions from a clean problem statement and gap analysis. Don Fedyk reminded the group that solution development is currently outside the WG charter.
* **WG Adoption Issues**: Lou Berger raised a procedural question in the chat regarding how open issues raised during the working group adoption of `draft-ietf-manet-inet-gap-analysis` will be reviewed and resolved, noting that the problem statement should remain solution-agnostic and that the original adoption comments need to be addressed by the authors. Don Fedyk confirmed the chairs and authors will revisit and resolve the adoption issues.
* **Cross-WG Coordination**: Fred Templin mentioned informal discussions with 6man Area Directors regarding solution work handling.

### Open Microphone Discussions
* **MANET and Delay-Tolerant Networking (DTN)**: Skyler Earl asked about the application of MANET in LEO satellite/non-terrestrial contexts and its relation to DTN (BPv7). Fred Templin and Ronald in 't Velt clarified that MANET assumes an end-to-end path exists at a given point in time (route-and-forward), whereas DTN operates on a store-carry-forward model for intermittently connected environments; thus, the two approaches are complementary.
* **Expired AODV Draft**: Abdussalam Baryun and Ronald in 't Velt discussed the status of the expired AODV draft by Charlie Perkins. Ronald in 't Velt noted that reviving the work would require addressing historical security and multihoming objections from 2015–2016 and re-chartering with the Area Director. Don Fedyk noted a lack of active author participation and critical mass for the work at present.

